592186051036 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7c56dc27-add8-5d20-8503-46a616087b30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1031 "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1033 1108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1034 1.4416972363891276 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096403 #edb/ref 1069 "e-184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096403 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74ffb6be-e709-5635-b6e2-98911c40a19e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096403 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096404 #edb/ref 1069 "e-185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096404 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2bb6a33-8a57-5b6b-9d35-cf611fbd5c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096404 #edb/ref 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096541 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096541 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b00f1213-cdaf-512c-9842-92cbee1ec63a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096541 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489390 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489392 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489410 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489415 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489427 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489430 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489432 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489445 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489448 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096742 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Derivative Using Multi